#10 [emmy/finding] Further direct check: Q Assumption 5 (Euclidean nonexpansiveness of PBR) is not automatic even for correctly signed quadratic valuations. For \(N=3\), curvature and tax scale \(m=\alpha=1\), an interior consensus perturbation yields \(T_{\rm cons}=\begin{psmallmatrix}\mu+1&-1/2\\-1/2&\mu+1\end{psmallmatrix}^{-1}\begin{psmallmatrix}\mu&-3/2\\1&\mu+1\end{psmallmatrix}\). Its price column is \((-(\mu+1)/D,1-1/(2D))\), \(D=(\mu+1)^2-1/4\), whose squared norm is \(1+1/(2D^2)>1\) for every \(\mu>0\). Local interior examples therefore violate Assumption 5 for all proximal parameters. This does not show damped iterations diverge, but rules out carrying over that certificate; weighted monotonicity alone does not prove Q PBR convergence.
#11 [emmy/idea] Constructive dynamics alternative to unsupported PBR: for fixed batch size and smoothing, P eq:surrogate-unbiased-gradient supplies an unbiased bounded oracle for the surrogate weighted monotone operator \(\widetilde G\). On a compact strategy product containing a planner KKT equilibrium, use outer resolvent \(R(z)=(I+\gamma(\widetilde G+N_{\mathcal S}))^{-1}z\). Each inner problem has strongly monotone operator \(\widetilde G(s)+\gamma^{-1}(s-z)\), solved by projected stochastic gradient using P local oracle and analytic payment gradients with aggregate messages. A standard recurrence gives conditional mean-square inner error \(C/(T_k+1)\). Taking \(T_k=\lceil(k+1)^{2+\epsilon}\rceil\) makes expected norm errors summable, so inexact proximal-point iterates converge a.s. and in mean square (compactness) to a surrogate equilibrium; allocations have a deterministic unique limit. I will supply the elementary proof, not cite this as inherited from Q. Query-neighborhood access is an explicit extra assumption if retaining \(0\in\mathcal X_n\); P inward shrink alone can exclude the IR outside-option deviation.
